        "\n",
        "## Resumen Estructurado — Congreso BiSide 2026\n",
        "\n",
        "**PREDICTORES CLÍNICOS Y COMORBILIDADES ASOCIADAS A DESENLACES HOSPITALARIOS EN PATOLOGÍA ENDOCRINO-METABÓLICA EN CHILE, 2019-2024**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Introducción:** Las enfermedades endocrinas, nutricionales y metabólicas generan una carga hospitalaria creciente en Chile, con perfiles de mortalidad y estadía heterogéneos entre categorías diagnósticas. Identificar predictores pronósticos diferenciados, ajustando por la estructura jerárquica de los establecimientos, resulta esencial para optimizar la gestión clínica intrahospitalaria.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Materiales y Métodos:** Estudio de cohorte retrospectiva sobre 128.052 egresos del sistema de Grupos Relacionados de Diagnóstico (GRD) de Chile (2019-2024), clasificados en diez categorías diagnósticas (CIE-10 E00-E89) y catorce grupos de comorbilidades derivadas de diagnósticos secundarios. Se ajustaron ecuaciones de estimación generalizadas (GEE) con agrupación por hospital y correlación intercambiable: enlace logit para mortalidad intrahospitalaria y enlace logarítmico con distribución binomial negativa para duración de estadía. La selección de comorbilidades se realizó mediante pruebas bivariadas independientes por categoría (p<0,05). Complementariamente, cinco algoritmos de clasificación fueron evaluados con seis técnicas de balanceo de clases y umbral optimizado por el índice J de Youden.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Resultados:** Se registraron 2.748 defunciones (mortalidad global 2,15%). Obesidad (32,2%) y diabetes (28,1%) constituyeron las categorías más frecuentes. La mortalidad osciló entre 0,13% (obesidad) y 4,47% (trastornos de glucosa), con diabetes en 2,00%, desnutrición 3,79% y trastornos metabólicos 3,60%. La estadía media varió de 2,9 días (obesidad) a 16,5 días (deficiencias nutricionales). Las GEE alcanzaron AUC de 0,955 (tiroides) a 0,767 (nutricionales) para mortalidad, con edad, severidad y riesgo de mortalidad como predictores transversales consistentes. Las comorbilidades nutricionales incrementaron la estadía en +8,0 días y las cardiovasculares en +7,2 días. El aprendizaje automático con balanceo logró AUC de hasta 0,982 (obesidad) y 0,855 (desnutrición).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Conclusiones:** La patología endocrino-metabólica presenta perfiles de riesgo marcadamente distintos por categoría, desde la baja letalidad de la obesidad hasta la elevada mortalidad de los trastornos glucémicos. El modelado GEE con estructura hospitalaria y selección específica de comorbilidades proporciona herramientas interpretables para la estratificación de riesgo clínico diferenciado.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Palabras clave:** enfermedades endocrinas, mortalidad intrahospitalaria, ecuaciones de estimación generalizadas, comorbilidad, aprendizaje automático.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note appearance=\"minimal\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Structured Abstract — BiSide 2026 Congress\n",
        "\n",
        "**CLINICAL PREDICTORS AND COMORBIDITIES ASSOCIATED WITH HOSPITAL OUTCOMES IN ENDOCRINE-METABOLIC PATHOLOGY IN CHILE, 2019-2024**\n",
        "\n",
        "**Introduction:** Endocrine, nutritional, and metabolic diseases impose a growing hospital burden in Chile, with heterogeneous mortality and length-of-stay profiles across diagnostic categories. Identifying differentiated prognostic predictors while accounting for the hierarchical structure of healthcare facilities is essential for optimizing in-hospital clinical management.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Materials and Methods:** Retrospective cohort study of 128,052 discharges from the Chilean Diagnosis-Related Groups (GRD) system (2019-2024), classified into ten diagnostic categories (ICD-10 E00-E89) and fourteen comorbidity groups derived from secondary diagnoses. Generalized estimating equations (GEE) were fitted with hospital clustering and exchangeable correlation: logit link for in-hospital mortality and log link with negative binomial distribution for length of stay. Comorbidity selection was performed through independent bivariate testing per category (p<0.05). Additionally, five classification algorithms were evaluated with six class balancing techniques and threshold optimized by Youden's J index.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Results:** A total of 2,748 deaths were recorded (overall mortality 2.15%). Obesity (32.2%) and diabetes (28.1%) were the most frequent categories. Mortality ranged from 0.13% (obesity) to 4.47% (glucose disorders), with diabetes at 2.00%, malnutrition 3.79%, and metabolic disorders 3.60%. Mean length of stay varied from 2.9 days (obesity) to 16.5 days (nutritional deficiencies). GEE achieved AUC from 0.955 (thyroid) to 0.767 (nutritional) for mortality, with age, severity, and mortality risk as consistent cross-cutting predictors. Nutritional comorbidities increased stay by +8.0 days and cardiovascular comorbidities by +7.2 days. Machine learning with class balancing achieved AUC up to 0.982 (obesity) and 0.855 (malnutrition).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Conclusions:** Endocrine-metabolic pathology exhibits markedly distinct risk profiles by category, from the low lethality of obesity to the elevated mortality of glucose disorders. GEE modeling with hospital structure and category-specific comorbidity selection provides interpretable tools for differentiated clinical risk stratification.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Keywords:** endocrine diseases, in-hospital mortality, generalized estimating equations, comorbidity, machine learning.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "# Methods\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.1 Study Design and Data Source\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a retrospective cohort study using GRD (Grupo Relacionado de Diagnóstico) public hospital discharge data from the Chilean Ministry of Health (MINSAL) for the years 2019–2024. The GRD database captures all hospitalizations in public hospitals and contains 35 diagnostic fields (DIAGNOSTICO1 through DIAGNOSTICO35), administrative variables, and clinical indicators including severity (IR_29301_SEVERIDAD: coded 1–3 as Low, Medium, High) and mortality risk (IR_29301_MORTALIDAD: coded 1–3 as Low, Medium, High). Additional variables include patient dates of birth and admission/discharge, sex, and service of admission.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.2 Case Identification\n",
        "\n",
        "Cases were identified through the primary diagnosis (DIAGNOSTICO1) field using ICD-10 codes E00–E89 (Endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases). Cases were classified into 10 disease categories based on anatomical and functional groupings:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **E00–E07**: Thyroid disorders\n",
        "- **E08–E13**: Diabetes mellitus (including drug-induced and secondary forms)\n",
        "- **E15–E16**: Other disorders of glucose regulation\n",
        "- **E20–E35**: Disorders of other endocrine glands (parathyroid, pituitary, adrenal, etc.)\n",
        "- **E36**: Intraoperative complications of endocrine procedures\n",
        "- **E40–E46**: Malnutrition and nutritional deficiencies\n",
        "- **E50–E64**: Other nutritional deficiency disorders\n",
        "- **E65–E68**: Obesity and hyperalimentation\n",
        "- **E70–E88**: Metabolic disorders (amino acid, lipid, carbohydrate metabolism)\n",
        "- **E89**: Postprocedural endocrine and metabolic complications\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.3 Lifestyle-Related Comorbidity Classification\n",
        "\n",
        "Comorbidities were identified from secondary diagnoses (DIAGNOSTICO2 through DIAGNOSTICO35) using ICD-10 codes and grouped into 14 lifestyle-related categories:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Obesity/Overweight**: E65, E66, E67, E68, R63, Z68\n",
        "2. **Dyslipidemia/Metabolic**: E78, E79, E88\n",
        "3. **Diabetes/Glucose disorders**: E08, E09, E10, E11, E12, E13, E14, R73\n",
        "4. **Hypertension**: I10, I11, I12, I13, I15, I16, I27\n",
        "5. **Tobacco-related**: F17, Z72, Z87, J40, J41, J42, J43, J44, J47, J98\n",
        "6. **Alcohol-related**: F10, K70, K29, G31, G62, G72, I42, K86, E24, E52, T51, Y90, Y91\n",
        "7. **Substance use**: F11, F12, F13, F14, F15, F16, F18, F19, F55\n",
        "8. **Nutritional deficiencies**: E40, E41, E42, E43, E44, E45, E46, E50, E51, E52, E53, E54, E55, E56, E58, E59, E60, E61, E63, E64, D50, D51, D52, D53\n",
        "9. **Liver disease (non-alcoholic)**: K72, K73, K74, K75, K76, K77\n",
        "10. **Sleep disorders**: G47, F51, G25\n",
        "11. **Cardiovascular disease**: I20, I21, I22, I23, I24, I25, I50, I51, I64, I65, I66, I67, I70, I73, I77, I78\n",
        "12. **Gastrointestinal disease**: K21, K22, K25, K26, K27, K28, K29, K30, K31, K58, K59, K90, K91, K92\n",
        "13. **Musculoskeletal/Sedentary**: M25, M54, M62, M79, M80, M81, M85\n",
        "14. **Mental health/Lifestyle**: F32, F33, F34, F41, F43, F48, F50, F54\n",
        "\n",
        "For each patient, a binary indicator $I_j$ denotes presence of comorbidity $j$, and total comorbidity burden is defined as:\n",
        "$$N_{\\text{comorbidities}} = \\sum_{j=1}^{14} I_j$$\n",
        "\n",
        "## 1.4 Statistical Analyses\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4.1 Descriptive Statistics\n",
        "\n",
        "Descriptive statistics were computed for age, sex, mortality, length of stay (LOS), and comorbidity burden. Categorical variables are presented as frequencies and percentages; continuous variables are presented with mean, standard deviation, median, and range. Mortality rate (in-hospital death) is presented as percentage. LOS is presented as mean, median, and interquartile range.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4.2 Bivariate Associations\n",
        "\n",
        "Chi-square tests were performed to assess associations between categorical variables and mortality:\n",
        "$$\\chi^2 = \\sum_{i,j} \\frac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $O_{ij}$ is the observed frequency and $E_{ij}$ is the expected frequency under independence. Fisher's exact test was used when expected cell frequencies were less than 5. Wilcoxon rank-sum test was used to compare LOS between groups:\n",
        "$$W = \\sum_{i=1}^{n_1} R_i$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $R_i$ are ranks assigned to observations from the first group.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4.3 Mixed-Effects Logistic Regression for Mortality\n",
        "\n",
        "A mixed-effects logistic regression model was fitted to examine associations between comorbidities and mortality, with year as a random effect:\n",
        "$$\\log\\left(\\frac{\\pi_{ij}}{1-\\pi_{ij}}\\right) = \\beta_0 + \\mathbf{x}_{ij}^T\\boldsymbol{\\beta} + u_j$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $\\pi_{ij}$ is the probability of mortality for individual $i$ in year $j$, $\\mathbf{x}_{ij}$ are fixed covariates, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ are fixed effect coefficients, and $u_j \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2_u)$ is the random year effect. Only comorbidities significant in bivariate analysis ($p < 0.05$) were included in the model. In Python, this was approximated using Generalized Estimating Equations (GEE) with logit link function and exchangeable correlation structure, with year as the grouping variable. Results are presented as Odds Ratios (OR) with 95% confidence intervals (CI).\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4.4 Negative Binomial Mixed Models for Length of Stay\n",
        "\n",
        "LOS was modeled using a negative binomial mixed regression model with year as a random effect:\n",
        "$$\\log(\\mu_{ij}) = \\beta_0 + \\mathbf{x}_{ij}^T\\boldsymbol{\\beta} + u_j$$\n",
        "$$Y_{ij} \\sim \\text{NB}(\\mu_{ij}, \\theta)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $Y_{ij}$ is the LOS for individual $i$ in year $j$, $\\mu_{ij}$ is the expected count, $\\theta$ is the dispersion parameter, and variance = $\\mu + \\mu^2/\\theta$. In Python, Negative Binomial regression was fitted with year as categorical predictor using statsmodels. Results are presented as Incidence Rate Ratios (IRR) with 95% CI.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4.5 Model Performance Assessment\n",
        "\n",
        "Model performance was assessed using the following metrics:\n",
        "\n",
        "**Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC)**:\n",
        "$$\\text{AUC} = P(S_1 > S_0)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $S_1$ and $S_0$ are predicted probabilities for positive and negative cases, respectively.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Hosmer-Lemeshow Goodness of Fit Test**:\n",
        "$$C = \\sum_{g=1}^{10} \\frac{(O_g - E_g)^2}{E_g(1 - E_g/n_g)}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $O_g$ and $E_g$ are observed and expected frequencies in decile $g$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4.6 Pseudo-R² Measures\n",
        "\n",
        "Multiple pseudo-$R^2$ measures were computed:\n",
        "\n",
        "**McFadden's $R^2$**:\n",
        "$$R^2_{\\text{McFadden}} = 1 - \\frac{\\log L_{\\text{full}}}{\\log L_{\\text{null}}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Cox-Snell $R^2$**:\n",
        "$$R^2_{\\text{Cox-Snell}} = 1 - \\left(\\frac{L_{\\text{null}}}{L_{\\text{full}}}\\right)^{2/n}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Nagelkerke $R^2$**:\n",
        "$$R^2_{\\text{Nagelkerke}} = \\frac{R^2_{\\text{Cox-Snell}}}{1 - L_{\\text{null}}^{2/n}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4.7 Additional Classification Metrics\n",
        "\n",
        "**Sensitivity** (True Positive Rate):\n",
        "$$\\text{Se} = \\frac{TP}{TP + FN}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Specificity** (True Negative Rate):\n",
        "$$\\text{Sp} = \\frac{TN}{TN + FP}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Positive Predictive Value**:\n",
        "$$\\text{PPV} = \\frac{TP}{TP + FP}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Negative Predictive Value**:\n",
        "$$\\text{NPV} = \\frac{TN}{TN + FN}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**F1 Score**:\n",
        "$$F_1 = 2 \\times \\frac{\\text{Precision} \\times \\text{Recall}}{\\text{Precision} + \\text{Recall}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Matthews Correlation Coefficient**:\n",
        "$$\\text{MCC} = \\frac{TP \\cdot TN - FP \\cdot FN}{\\sqrt{(TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN)}}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "**Brier Score**:\n",
        "$$\\text{BS} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(p_i - o_i)^2$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $p_i$ is predicted probability and $o_i$ is observed outcome.\n",
        "\n",
        "For length of stay regression models: Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), Mean Absolute Error (MAE), and R² were computed.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1.4.8 Sensitivity Analyses\n",
        "\n",
        "Youden's index was calculated to determine optimal threshold for binary classification:\n",
        "$$J = \\max(\\text{Se} + \\text{Sp} - 1)$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Machine learning models were evaluated using 80/20 stratified train-test split with feature standardization.\n",
        "\n",
